 This install applies to "X-Wing" 1.0, an unofficial Amiga port of the 1993
 PC game by LucasArts and Totally Games.

 THIS IS AN EARLY ALPHA. The port runs the whole game and is partly
 optimized. Please read "What to expect" before installing.


 What to expect:
 ---------------
 What has been checked: the port's code was run in an emulated A1200 with a
 68030 and compared step by step against the PC original running under
 DOSBox-X, at these points of the game: the start, the LucasArts logo, the
 title, pilot registration, the concourse with the pilot saved, the Pilot
 Proving Ground, Historical Combat, the Tour of Duty, the tech room, the
 film room, a training flight, a mission flight, a Tour of Duty battle,
 playing back recorded films (a combat film among them) and the exit to
 DOS. At each point the screen, the memory and every call the game made
 matched the original.

 What to expect when playing:
 - Speed: the port is partly optimized. Expect the intro to run slowly
   on a 68030, its Star Destroyer scene above all. Flight is much faster
   than in 0.2, though busy scenes with many ships still slow down. Making
   it faster is still the port's current step.
 - Unsupported paths: the port stops rather than guessing when the game
   does something it does not support yet. The tests cover the paths
   above, not every mission or menu choice. Under WHDLoad a stop shows the
   requester "X-Wing stopped"; started from a Shell (cd to the drawer's
   data, then X-Wing), the Shell shows a line beginning with XWING_STOP.
   Please report it with that line and what you were doing (see Contact).
 - Saving: pilots and films are written as on the PC, but saving under
   WHDLoad has not been tested yet. Keep copies of pilot files you care
   about.
 - Machines: tested only in an emulated A1200 with a 68030, not yet on
   real hardware, on a 68040 or 68060, or with WHDLoad's MMU option.
 - Memory: started directly it runs with 8 MB Fast RAM; under WHDLoad it
   needs more (see Starting).
 - Sound: the game is set up as on a PC with a Sound Blaster: AdLib
   music and sound effects, and the digitized speech, play on the Amiga.
   On a 68030 the intro runs slower than on the PC, so some of its spoken
   lines are cut short or overlap.
 - Input: keyboard and mouse. No joystick yet.
 - The copy-protection question is skipped: the game continues as if it
   had been answered correctly.


 Requirements:
 -------------
 A PAL or NTSC Amiga with AGA, Kickstart 3.0 or newer and a 68020 or
 better; a 68030 or faster is recommended. 2 MB Chip RAM and 8 MB Fast RAM
 to start the game directly; to start it with WHDLoad, WHDLoad version 17.0
 or better, more than 8 MB Fast RAM and a Kickstart image (see below).


 Starting:
 ---------
 The X-Wing drawer has two icons. Both run the same program.

 X-Wing starts the game directly, from Workbench. It needs less memory:
 8 MB Fast RAM is enough, and no WHDLoad or Kickstart image. The game takes
 over the machine while it runs; quit from the game's menus.

 X-Wing (WHDLoad) starts it with WHDLoad, which reserves 2 MB ChipMem and
 8.5 MB OtherMem, including its Kickstart image, so it needs more than 8 MB
 Fast RAM: for example 16 MB. Help quits at any time and Workbench comes
 back as it was. The data cache needs the MMU: WHDLoad uses it on a 68040
 or 68060, on a 68030 only with the WHDLoad option MMU (add the tooltype
 MMU to the icon).

 For WHDLoad, the game requires an installed A600/A1200/A4000 Kickstart
 3.1 image. The kickstart image must be located in the directory
 "Devs:Kickstarts" and must be named "kick40063.A600", "kick40068.A1200" or
 "kick40068.A4000". Additionally the file "kick40063.A600.RTB",
 "kick40068.A1200.RTB" or "kick40068.A4000.RTB" must be installed in the same
 directory as the kickstart image. It can be found in the archive
 "util/boot/skick346.lha" on aminet.


 Installation:
 -------------
 The install does not include the original game files. It installs them from
 the five 1.44 MB PC floppies of X-Wing 1.0 (LucasArts, 1993). Other
 releases, such as the Collector's CD-ROM, are not supported. Run the Install
 icon and select a drawer containing either the disk images

   disk1.img  disk2.img  disk3.img  disk4.img  disk5.img

 or the files XWING.001 to XWING.005 copied from the disks, together with
 INSTALL.EXE, DETECT.EXE and READ.ME from disk 1. Every file is checked;
 damaged or other disks are refused. The game is installed as the original
 installer sets it up for a Sound Blaster, with digitized speech.

 To update, select the same parent drawer and choose Keep. Use existing (the
 default) keeps the installed game files; Replace installs them again from the
 disks. Pilots and films are kept either way.

 Version 0.1 installed into a drawer named XWing, with the game files set up
 for AdLib sound. Selecting the same parent drawer installs X-Wing beside it,
 with the game files from the disks, and copies the pilots (.PLT) and films
 (.CLP) over. The old XWing drawer can then be deleted.


 Playing:
 --------
 Keyboard and mouse work as in the PC original; keys are at their US
 keyboard positions. See the original manual for the controls. There is no
 joystick support yet. Quit from the game's menus. Under WHDLoad, the
 QuitKey (Help) quits immediately.


 History:
 --------
 version 0.3 (25.11.2026):
 - faster: the translated code is partly optimized; on a 68030 a busy
   combat film plays at 240 ms per frame, 320 ms in 0.2, and flight is
   much faster; the intro is still slow

 version 0.2 (23.11.2026):
 - digitized speech: the voices of the PC version with a Sound Blaster
   now play, in the intro, the menus and the Tour of Duty
 - the game starts without WHDLoad from the new X-Wing icon, with 8 MB
   Fast RAM; X-Wing (WHDLoad) starts it with WHDLoad
 - installed into an X-Wing drawer, set up for a Sound Blaster; updating
   from 0.1 installs the game files again from the disks and copies the
   pilots and films from the old XWing drawer

 version 0.1 (19.11.2026):
 - initial early alpha release: the whole game, with music and sound
   effects, unoptimized


